{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe over-optimism: The model consistently forecasts a slow, linear upward drift (mean reversion to ~85-90) while the true market exhibits sharp, volatile mean reversion to lower levels (76-80) following price spikes above 85.",
    "Apply aggressive downward adjustment after spikes: When history shows a peak above 85 followed by a drop, the model fails to anticipate the depth of the correction; reduce forecasts by 5-10 EUR immediately to align with the observed crash to the mid-70s.",
    "Ignore sentiment lag: Positive sentiment signals (0.20) in the history do not trigger the model's expected price increase; the model remains stuck in a 'flat' trajectory regardless of sentiment, so do not rely on sentiment to justify upward corrections.",
    "Adjust for horizon-specific lag: The model's 30-day forecast is too smooth and lags the true volatility; apply a 'step-down' correction for the first 10-15 days of the forecast horizon to match the actual rapid decline, then allow for a slower recovery in the final 15 days.",
    "Treat the 85-90 range as a resistance ceiling: The model assumes prices will hover or rise in the 85-90 range; historically, this range acts as a temporary peak before a significant drop to the 76-78 support level."
  ]
}
